# Synthetic fixture: what `operation` resets, and what it deliberately does not.
# The fake shifts x and y by `shift` forward and unshifts them inverse.
#
#  block 0  widens the tolerance to 100 m and reverses the direction, then
#           asserts twice -- the second pair proves both are sticky.
#  block 1  the same 14.14 deviation with no tolerance of its own: the reset to
#           0.5 mm turns it into a failure. Direction-independent by design, so
#           this pins the tolerance reset and nothing else.
#  block 2  tolerance 1 m and no direction: passes only if the direction is back
#           to forward, since an inverse would be 28.28 m out.
#  block 3  no `accept` at all. Must reuse block 2's, because gie's operation()
#           never clears T.a.
<gie-strict>
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
operation  fake shift=10
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
tolerance  100 m
direction  inverse
accept     1 2
expect     1 2
accept     5 6
expect     5 6
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
operation  fake shift=10
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
accept     3 4
expect     3 4
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
operation  fake shift=10
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
tolerance  1 m
accept     1 2
expect     11 12
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
operation  fake shift=0
-------------------------------------------------------------------------------
expect     1 2
</gie-strict>
